# The Story: A Restaurant for the Dead (or the Dying)

This song tells the story of a **restaurant in the afterlife** — a waystation for souls who have died, disguised as a charming little forest eatery.

## The Clues

**The impossible journey:** Following acorns (ドングリ) never gets you there — because it's not a place you can find by searching. You arrive only when you've been "forgotten" (忘れた人から辿り着く) — i.e., when you've died, or when the living world has let you go. The empty pockets suggest the dead carry nothing with them.

**The empty restaurant:** No reservations, all seats empty at lunchtime — because the dead don't arrive on a schedule. The little birds laughing (パタパタ) adds eerie whimsy: nature observing the souls passing through.

**The "recommendation only" menu:** The dead don't get to choose their fate. The kitchen music — violin, flute, cello, viola — is a **funeral quartet** repurposed as restaurant ambience.

**The chorus:** "Eat your fill, then go to sleep" (お眠りよ) — the final rest. "Goodbye everyone, tomorrow is another day, et cetera" — a gentle, almost dismissive farewell to existence itself. The "et cetera" (エトセトラ) is darkly playful: whatever comes after doesn't matter much.

**The finale:** The frantic right-to-left movement (おおわらわ) is the staff rushing souls along. The baby bear dancing on a palm — a soul's last performance? The full course list ends with **"no dessert"** — nothing sweet remains after death. And the kicker:

> **"We'll deliver it all the way into your grave — tonight is your final full course."**

## The Tone

The lighthearted, mischievous melody is exactly right: this is death rendered as a whimsical, slightly cheeky hospitality experience. The restaurant serves you your last meal, tucks you in, and waves goodbye — the afterlife as a charming little business that's seen countless customers and treats each death with cheerful, practiced routine. [end of text]


